---
title: Building
sidebar:
  order: 1
---

After making changes, you need to build the ROM to test them.

## Using VS Code

With Visual Studio Code focused, press **Ctrl+Shift+B** (Cmd+Shift+B on macOS) to build the ROM.

If you set up your project [using Nix](/papermario-dx/dx), use **Ctrl+Shift+P > Tasks: Run Task > Build Mod (Nix)** instead, so the build runs inside the Nix environment.

The output ROM is at `ver/us/build/papermario.z64`. See [Running](#running) below for how to load it.

## Building manually

If you prefer to build from the command line, or if the VS Code task isn't working:

{% tabs %}
{% tabitem label="Windows" %}
Open a terminal in the project folder and run:

```
.\tools\configure.bat
.\tools\build.bat
```

`configure.bat` downloads the compiler toolchain (on the first run) and creates a `build.ninja` file. You only need to re-run it if you add or remove source files. `build.bat` builds the ROM.
{% /tabitem %}
{% tabitem label="macOS/Linux" %}
Make sure `git`, `ninja`, and `python3` are installed, then run:

```sh
./tools/configure.sh
./tools/build.sh
```

`configure.sh` downloads the compiler toolchain (on the first run) and creates a `build.ninja` file. You only need to re-run it if you add or remove source files. `build.sh` builds the ROM.
{% /tabitem %}
{% tabitem label="Using Nix" %}
```sh
nix develop
```

This builds (or downloads, if cached) the cross-compiler toolchain, build tools, and all other dependencies using [Nix](https://nixos.org). You need to be in this shell for the following commands to work.

```sh
./tools/configure.sh
ninja
```
{% /tabitem %}
{% /tabs %}

{% aside %}
If you're experiencing a weird build issue (e.g. after updating DX), try `./tools/configure.sh --clean` (or `.\tools\configure.bat --clean` on Windows) to delete previously-built files and start fresh.
{% /aside %}

## Running

Load `ver/us/build/papermario.z64` in an emulator. We recommend the accurate emulator [ares](https://ares-emu.net). For real hardware, copy this ROM to a flashcart.

{% aside type="caution" %}
If you don't regularly test with ares and/or real hardware, your mod may not work correctly for others.
{% /aside %}
